WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:05.430
In 2018, something happened that shattered
every truism hollywood has held dear for the

00:00:05.430 --> 00:00:11.400
past… ever. — Black Panther, a story set
in Africa with a mostly-black cast, became

00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:16.360
the highest grossing domestic film of the
year, beating even Avengers: Infinity War.

00:00:16.360 --> 00:00:21.249
And while many people may have heard the term
“Afrofuturism” before Black Panther took

00:00:21.249 --> 00:00:26.680
the world by storm, suddenly, it solidified
Afrofuturism’s entry into the mainstream.

00:00:26.680 --> 00:00:30.869
But while Black Panther is a milestone for
Afrofuturism, it certainly isn’t the sum

00:00:30.869 --> 00:00:35.220
total of the genre. Later the same year, Tomi
Adeyemi’s Children of Blood and Bone

00:00:35.220 --> 00:00:38.940
topped the New York Times Hardcover Best Sellers
List, and Janelle Monáe, who has been dabbling

00:00:38.940 --> 00:00:43.230
in the genre, both musically and aesthetically,
for over a decade, released their 3rd album,

00:00:43.230 --> 00:00:47.160
Dirty Computer.

00:00:47.420 --> 00:00:52.020
But Afrofuturism doesn’t simply mean “Science
fiction” plus Africa--it’s a bit more

00:00:52.030 --> 00:00:56.980
nuanced than that. Afrofuturism addresses
the cultural issues and concerns of the African

00:00:56.980 --> 00:01:02.030
diaspora through technoculture and science
fiction, which is what Black Panther does.

00:01:02.030 --> 00:01:06.240
But you can’t just wrap a lightsaber in
kente cloth and call it Afrofuturism (unless

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:08.040
you’re John

00:01:08.040 --> 00:01:11.569
Boyega). Afrofuturism, like
rap, reggae, jazz, blues and all the music

00:01:11.569 --> 00:01:16.970
to come out of black experience, is about
creating art out of pain, strength, loss and

00:01:16.970 --> 00:01:22.440
successes. It is fundamentally rooted in being
denied a full history and looking to the future

00:01:22.440 --> 00:01:23.609
to correct that.

00:01:23.609 --> 00:01:28.590
In his 1994 essay “Black to the Future,”,
author and lecturer Mark Dairy asks: “Can

00:01:28.590 --> 00:01:33.409
a community whose past has been deliberately
rubbed out, and whose energies have subsequently

00:01:33.409 --> 00:01:39.039
been consumed by the search for legible traces
of its history, imagine possible futures?”

00:01:39.040 --> 00:01:40.760
And this is where Afrofuturism comes in.

00:01:40.760 --> 00:01:43.360
(It’s Lit intro)

00:01:43.360 --> 00:01:49.460
At the root of Afrofuturism is black speculative
fiction, which uses science fiction as a way

00:01:49.469 --> 00:01:54.139
of telling stories about black resistance,
history and hope. According to sociologist

00:01:54.139 --> 00:01:59.659
Alondra Nelson, who convened an early online
forum on Afrofuturism, said: “Afrofuturism

00:01:59.659 --> 00:02:06.179
has emerged as a term of convenience to describe
analysis, criticism and cultural production

00:02:06.179 --> 00:02:11.009
that addresses the intersections between race
and technology… Part of the resilience of

00:02:11.009 --> 00:02:16.110
black culture and black life is about imagining
the impossible, imagining a better place,

00:02:16.110 --> 00:02:21.630
a different world… Afrofuturism draws upon
the feeling of alienation inherited from the

00:02:21.630 --> 00:02:27.880
slavery of American blacks, which it sublimates.
In this conception, certain elements of Afro-American

00:02:27.880 --> 00:02:34.090
culture (such as the transcendence of spirituals)
are re-imagined and transposed into a new

00:02:34.090 --> 00:02:38.970
cosmic and legendary perspective, where the
alienated becomes extraterrestrial.”

00:02:38.970 --> 00:02:45.130
In an essay written by N. K. Jemisin from
2013 titled “How Long ‘til Black Future

00:02:45.130 --> 00:02:49.660
Month?” (which later became the title of
her 2018 short story collection), she discusses

00:02:49.660 --> 00:02:55.760
rewatching the old Hanna-Bar-BEAR-a cartoon
The Jetsons and being struck at how homogenous

00:02:55.760 --> 00:03:00.420
the series’ depiction of an idyllic future
is, with no people of color in it.

00:03:00.420 --> 00:03:05.971
According to Jemisin: “There is nobody even
slightly brown in the Jetsons’ world. Even

00:03:05.971 --> 00:03:12.700
the family android sounds white. This is supposed
to be the real world’s future, right? Albeit

00:03:12.700 --> 00:03:19.350
in silly, humorous form. Thing is, not-white-people
make up most of the world’s population,

00:03:19.350 --> 00:03:23.350
now as well as back in the Sixties when the
show was created. So what happened to all

00:03:23.350 --> 00:03:27.620
those people, in the minds of this show’s
creators? Are they down beneath the clouds,

00:03:27.620 --> 00:03:32.630
where the Jetsons never go? Was there an apocalypse,
or maybe a pogrom?”

00:03:32.630 --> 00:03:37.320
Although the mainstreamification of Afrofuturism
is relatively new, its themes have appeared

00:03:37.320 --> 00:03:42.220
in literature for over a century. In 1899,
Charles W. Chesnutt published a collection

00:03:42.220 --> 00:03:46.370
of short stories called The Conjure Woman,
which discussed themes of black resistance

00:03:46.370 --> 00:03:51.110
and revenge against antebellum culture using
traditional African American spirituality.

00:03:51.110 --> 00:03:56.480
Beginning in 1902, Of One Blood was serially
published by Pauline Hopkins, which talks

00:03:56.480 --> 00:04:01.610
about a man who goes on an expedition to Ethiopia
and discovers a hidden, technologically advanced

00:04:01.610 --> 00:04:07.020
civilization. It’s basically Wakanda, before
Wakanda.. The novel also explores the protagonist’s

00:04:07.020 --> 00:04:11.900
own anti-blackness and ignorance towards black
history. He goes to Ethiopia to find treasure,

00:04:11.900 --> 00:04:14.650
but instead finds it in the form of his own
identity.

00:04:14.650 --> 00:04:20.049
In the 1920s, W. E. B. Du Bois wrote among
the first post-apocalyptic short fiction with

00:04:20.049 --> 00:04:25.100
black subjects called The Comet. In the story,
Du Bois writes about a black man named Jim

00:04:25.100 --> 00:04:30.710
Davis, the only man left alive after a comet
hits New York City and unleashes toxic gases

00:04:30.710 --> 00:04:36.580
that kill everyone except himself and a white
woman named Julia. Jim rises as the symbol

00:04:36.580 --> 00:04:41.920
of the savior and hero of the narrative, a
new Adam in this destroyed New York.

00:04:41.920 --> 00:04:48.190
In the book Afrofuturism: The World of Black
Sci-Fi and Fantasy Culture, Ytasha L. Womack,

00:04:48.190 --> 00:04:52.411
a leading Afrofuturist, explains that the
story is one of the first to portray that

00:04:52.411 --> 00:04:57.550
“blackness is not a mark of shame but a
source of pride, establishing a space where

00:04:57.550 --> 00:04:59.750
black culture can thrive.”

00:04:59.750 --> 00:05:04.900
No conversation about Afrofuturism would be
complete without Octavia E. Butler, widely

00:05:04.900 --> 00:05:10.620
considered the “grand dame” of science
fiction today. Starting with the release of

00:05:10.620 --> 00:05:16.340
her first book Patternmaster in 1976, Butler
went on to release a dozen science fiction

00:05:16.340 --> 00:05:21.580
novels featuring Afrofuturist themes. While
she wasn’t as appreciated in her time, renewed

00:05:21.580 --> 00:05:26.680
interest in Afrofuturism has led to a surge
of new readers of her work. Her books are

00:05:26.680 --> 00:05:31.230
now often included in many high school and
college required reading lists. That Butler

00:05:31.230 --> 00:05:35.669
is only recently getting her due reflects
how slow the mainstream was to take interest

00:05:35.669 --> 00:05:38.740
in black fiction. But hey, we’re getting
there.

00:05:38.740 --> 00:05:44.360
Noted modern works that fall under the Afrofuturist
umbrella include Nnedi Okorafor’s Who Fears

00:05:44.360 --> 00:05:49.251
Death and Binti. Binti is a novella about
a girl that overcomes humble beginnings and

00:05:49.251 --> 00:05:54.130
is offered a place at Oomza University, one
of the most prestigious institutions of higher

00:05:54.130 --> 00:05:59.150
learning in the galaxy, but a great deal of
the series focuses on Binti trying to reconcile

00:05:59.150 --> 00:06:03.139
her heritage with her own natural intelligence
and personal goals.

00:06:03.139 --> 00:06:07.160
An Unkindness of Ghosts by Rivers Solomon,
who was a Campbell Award nominee for Best

00:06:07.160 --> 00:06:12.520
New Writer in 2018, explores how sustained
acts of abuse and violence can shape a culture

00:06:12.520 --> 00:06:17.380
and people. This one takes place on a generation
ship that is not only heavily stratified based

00:06:17.380 --> 00:06:22.730
on race, but also extremely restrictive of
any nonconformity, especially with regard

00:06:22.730 --> 00:06:29.020
to gender. It is, effectively, coping with
the violence of colonialism in spaaaaaace.

00:06:29.020 --> 00:06:33.260
Afrofuturist storytellers take advantage of
the speculative nature of the genre by simultaneously

00:06:33.260 --> 00:06:37.590
creating new worlds and deconstructing the
racial politics of this one. One of the criticisms

00:06:37.590 --> 00:06:42.400
lodged against dystopian fiction like Margaret
Atwood’s The Handmaid’s Tale or Suzanne

00:06:42.400 --> 00:06:46.360
Collins' The Hunger Games trilogy is that,
even in the worst of futures, there is no

00:06:46.360 --> 00:06:51.080
place for black bodies because either they
were banished or just seemed to disappear.

00:06:51.080 --> 00:06:54.890
This is why it’s important to recognize
the thematic elements of Afrofuturism that

00:06:54.890 --> 00:07:01.270
go beyond the idea that “Afrofuturism is
just science fiction plus Africa.” Afrofuturism

00:07:01.270 --> 00:07:05.750
is about reclaiming our past in order to craft
our future. And that is done by infusing black

00:07:05.750 --> 00:07:10.690
sound and stories with a forward thinking
desire to see how our culture could have evolved

00:07:10.690 --> 00:07:13.650
or could still evolve unhindered by colonial
influence.

00:07:13.650 --> 00:07:19.560
Here’s how author N. K. Jemisin put it as
part of a panel in 2016: “I think one of

00:07:19.560 --> 00:07:23.440
the most radical things that anyone in this
world can do is imagine that black people

00:07:23.440 --> 00:07:28.370
have a future.” Through Afrofuturism, black
society has a future that is beyond the clouds,

00:07:28.370 --> 00:07:30.290
just like everyone else.

00:07:30.290 --> 00:07:35.180
One final question we should ask: is “Afrofuturism”
too broad a term?

00:07:35.180 --> 00:07:39.720
Some African authors like Okorafor reject
the title of Afrofuturism because they do

00:07:39.720 --> 00:07:45.120
not write through the perspective of Western
Blackness. Okorafor is a Nigerian-American

00:07:45.120 --> 00:07:49.690
who is deeply connected to African culture,
and she explains in her TED Talk how she writes

00:07:49.690 --> 00:07:51.949
her science fiction through that lens:

00:07:51.949 --> 00:07:56.900
“I can best explain the difference between
classic science fiction and Afrofuturism if

00:07:56.900 --> 00:08:01.900
I used the octopus analogy. Like humans, octopuses
are some of the most intelligent creatures

00:08:01.900 --> 00:08:06.759
on earth. However, octopus intelligence evolved
from a different evolutionary line, separate

00:08:06.759 --> 00:08:10.500
from that of human beings, so the foundation
is different. The same can be said about the

00:08:10.500 --> 00:08:16.380
foundations of various forms of science fiction…
In non-Western culture, the mystical coexisting

00:08:16.380 --> 00:08:19.150
with the mundane is normal.”

00:08:19.150 --> 00:08:23.961
Okorafor also points to the history of Afrofuturism
being associated with music, especially in

00:08:23.961 --> 00:08:29.270
the United States “with African artists
as an afterthought.” In short: “I understand

00:08:29.270 --> 00:08:35.419
the necessity of it, I understand the uses
of it, but I do not consider myself an Afrofuturist.”

00:08:35.419 --> 00:08:41.450
As we enter a post-Black Panther era, Afrofuturism
is not just a cultural aesthetic, philosophy

00:08:41.450 --> 00:08:47.050
of science, and philosophy of history, but
also a money making machine. Disney, Marvel

00:08:47.050 --> 00:08:51.760
and Ryan Coogler turned the story of the first
black comic book superhero into a film that

00:08:51.760 --> 00:08:57.660
became the twelfth highest-grossing film of
all time with over $1.35 billion worldwide.

00:08:57.660 --> 00:09:02.430
Coogler took the story and infused it with
the kind of narrative that is at the heart

00:09:02.430 --> 00:09:08.240
of Afrofuturism — the possibility that Africa
and its descendants could have a powerful

00:09:08.240 --> 00:09:12.570
present and future — and made it immensely
entertaining and marketable.

00:09:12.570 --> 00:09:18.990
It will be exciting to see Afrofuturism grow
and expand as more black authors tackle the

00:09:18.990 --> 00:09:24.490
questions, claim the aesthetic and build on
its core idea: that not only do black people

00:09:24.490 --> 00:09:28.910
across the world have a future, but it’s
going to be pretty lit because we created

00:09:28.910 --> 00:09:29.700
it for ourselves.

